From: System Electronics
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 10:20:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] phy: cdns-dphy: drop the pm_runtime calls from power_on and power_off

Mainline cdns-dphy.c has no pm_runtime call anywhere. Ours called
pm_runtime_get_sync(), pm_runtime_set_active() and pm_runtime_enable()
on &phy->dev in power_on, and pm_runtime_put_sync(),
pm_runtime_set_suspended() and pm_runtime_disable() in power_off.

They had no effect on the hardware. phy_create() enables runtime PM on
&phy->dev only if the parent platform device has it enabled, and ours
does not: runtime_status of 7c01a000.dphy reads "unsupported" even on a
clean boot. So &phy->dev has no runtime PM callbacks and those calls only
moved bookkeeping. They did have one side effect: once power_on had
enabled runtime PM on &phy->dev, the phy core's own
phy_pm_runtime_get_sync() and phy_pm_runtime_put() stopped returning
-ENOTSUPP and started acting on it. Nothing needs that state.

esc_clk is kept, in all four places. It is a Hailo addition that
mainline does not have, and its clk_disable_unprepare() in power_off has
to stay: removing it along with the pm_runtime calls would leave a clock
enabled after every power cycle, the same kind of imbalance this series
is removing.

---
--- a/drivers/phy/cadence/cdns-dphy.c
+++ b/drivers/phy/cadence/cdns-dphy.c
@@ -327,10 +327,6 @@
 	if (!dphy->is_configured || dphy->is_powered)
 		return -EINVAL;
 
-	pm_runtime_get_sync(&phy->dev);
-	pm_runtime_set_active(&phy->dev);
-	pm_runtime_enable(&phy->dev);
-
 	clk_prepare_enable(dphy->psm_clk);
 	clk_prepare_enable(dphy->pll_ref_clk);
 	clk_prepare_enable(dphy->esc_clk);
@@ -356,10 +352,6 @@
 	reg = readl(dphy->regs + DPHY_CMN_SSM);
 	writel(reg & ~DPHY_CMN_SSM_EN, dphy->regs + DPHY_CMN_SSM);
 	dphy->is_powered = false;
-
-	pm_runtime_put_sync(&phy->dev);
-	pm_runtime_set_suspended(&phy->dev);
-	pm_runtime_disable(&phy->dev);
 	return 0;
 }
 
